1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,872
How do you tell the world about a payment without ever losing the news, or inventing it?

2
00:00:04,922 --> 00:00:08,952
Picture an app that saves a payment as paid, and then tells the shop.

3
00:00:09,002 --> 00:00:12,192
If it crashes in between, the shop never hears.

4
00:00:12,242 --> 00:00:16,392
Swap the order, and a crash announces a payment that was never saved.

5
00:00:16,442 --> 00:00:24,672
An office solves this with an outbox tray: you close the file and drop the letter in the tray in one motion, and the postman collects from the tray.

6
00:00:24,722 --> 00:00:31,680
When a payment ends, its news is written to the outbox in the same moment as its final state: both or neither.

7
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:39,936
A relay is the postman: it collects the oldest unsent news and passes it on, one payment's news always in order.

8
00:00:39,986 --> 00:00:46,680
It marks a letter sent only after it is delivered, so a crash can send one twice, never zero times.

9
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:51,528
Every piece of news has its own id, so a repeat is easy to drop.

10
00:00:51,578 --> 00:00:54,720
Next: delivering the news to the merchant's server.
